<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Identify and classify the critical points of the function</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Let&nbsp;\(\displaystyle f(x) = {@fx@}\).&nbsp;</p><p>i. Identify and classify the critical points of the function. Enter your answers as points in the relevant boxes and if there are none simply type in the word "none".</p><p>Maximum: \( \big( \) [[input:ans1]],&nbsp;[[input:ans2]] \( \big) \)<br></p><p>[[validation:ans1]]&nbsp;[[validation:ans2]]&nbsp;[[feedback:prt1]]&nbsp;[[feedback:prt2]]<br></p><p>Minimum: \( \big( \) [[input:ans3]],&nbsp;[[input:ans4]] \( \big) \)</p><p>[[validation:ans3]]&nbsp;[[validation:ans4]]&nbsp;[[feedback:prt3]]&nbsp;[[feedback:prt4]]</p><p>Stationary point of inflection: \( \big( \) [[input:ans5]],&nbsp;[[input:ans6]] \( \big) \)</p><p>[[validation:ans5]]&nbsp;[[validation:ans6]]&nbsp;[[feedback:prt5]]&nbsp;[[feedback:prt6]]</p><p>ii. Determine the intervals when the function is increasing and decreasing. Give your answers as intervals written as inequalities.</p><p></p><p>Increasing: [[input:ans7]] [[validation:ans7]] [[feedback:prt7]]</p><p>Decreasing: [[input:ans8]] [[validation:ans8]] [[feedback:prt8]]</p><p></p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>
<p>(i) To find stationary points we need to find the values of \(x\) for which \(f'(x) = 0\).</p><p>\(f'(x) = {@dfx@} = 0\)</p><p>\({@3*(x-aa)*(x-bb)@} = 0\)</p><p>\(x={@x1@}\) or \(x={@x2@}\).</p><p>We now need to classify these points. To do so we use the following table:</p>
<table>
<caption></caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">\(x=\)</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">before {@x1@}</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@x1@}</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">after {@x1@}</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">before {@x2@}<br></th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@x2@}<br></th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">after {@x2@}<br></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">\(f'(x)=\)</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(bx1,2)@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">0</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(ax1,2)@}<br></td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(bx2,2)@}<br></td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">0</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(ax2,2)@}<br></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; "><br></td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@behbx1@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">stationary</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@behax1@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@behbx2@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">stationary</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@behax2@}</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Since before \(x= {@x1@}\) the function is {@behbx1@} and after is {@behax1@}, there is a {@classx1@} at \(x={@x1@}\). Similarly, since before \(x= {@x2@}\) the function is {@behbx2@} and after is {@behax2@}, there is a {@classx2@} at \(x={@x2@}\).</p>All that is left to do is find the \(y-\)coordinates of each point as follows:</p>
<p>Since \(f({@x1@})={@ev(at(fx,[x=x1]),simp)@}\) and \(f({@x2@})={@ev(at(fx,[x=x2]),simp)@}\), thus </p>
<p>Maximum:\(\left ({@tamaxx@},{@tamaxy@}\right )\)</p>
<p>Minimum: \(\left ({@taminx@},{@taminy@}\right )\)</p>
<hr>
<p>Stationary point of inflection:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle f''(x)={@dfxs@}\) and \({@dfxs=0@} \Leftrightarrow x = {@sol_dfxs@}\)</p>
<p>\({@dfxs=0@}\) is negative up to \(x={@sol_dfxs@} \),  positive from there onwards.</p> <p>The stationary point of infection is thus \( ({@sol_dfxs@},{@ev(at(fx,[x=sol_dfxs]),simp)@}) \)</p>
<hr>
<p>(ii) The increasing and decreasing intervals can be obtained directly from the table above.</p>
<p> Increasing intervals: \({@tainc@}\)
<p> Decreasing intervals: \({@tadec@}\)]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
